},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"later vertices in ascending index. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating again gives the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d) bitset words per clique for degeneracy d."}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/BronKerboschEvents":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/BronKerboschEvents","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The Bron–Kerbosch algorithm with pivoting as a sequence of "},{"code":"BronKerboschEvent","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"s: the search of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"maximalCliques()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", one clique at a time. Pulling an event past the last one kept runs the"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"search to its next maximal clique and keeps the events on the way.","type":"text"}],"title":"BronKerboschEvents","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/bronkerboschevents","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"BronKerboschEvents","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BronKerboschEvents","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/TrussNumbers":{"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TrussNumbers","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The truss decomposition of an undirected graph (Cohen; Wang–Cheng), over its simple graph. An"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge’s truss number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every edge is in at"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"least k − 2 triangles: 2 for an edge in no triangle, k for an edge of a k-clique. Parallel"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edges share their simple edge’s number, and a self-loop has none."}],"title":"TrussNumbers","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/trussnumbers","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TrussNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"TrussNumbers","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/OnionLayers":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/OnionLayers","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"OnionLayers"}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"OnionLayers"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The onion decomposition of an undirected graph’s simple graph: each vertex’s layer, a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"refinement of the core decomposition in which every layer lies inside one k-shell. Layers are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"numbered from 1; isolated vertices, when there are any, are layer 1 alone."}],"type":"topic","title":"OnionLayers","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/onionlayers"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/ClusteringCoefficients":{"title":"ClusteringCoefficients","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/clusteringcoefficients","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"Triangles and clustering for every vertex of an undirected graph’s simple graph, from one","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pass.","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ClusteringCoefficients","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ClusteringCoefficients","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/ClusteringCoefficients"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/CoreNumbers":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The core decomposition of an undirected graph (Batagelj–Zaversnik), over its simple graph. A"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertex’s core number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every vertex has at"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"least k neighbors."}],"type":"topic","title":"CoreNumbers","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/corenumbers","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/CoreNumbers","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques":{"title":"Cliques","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques","type":"topic","role":"collection","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques":{"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MaximalCliques"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MaximalCliques"}],"title":"MaximalCliques","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques","abstract":[{"text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering"},{"text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"degeneracy d.","type":"text"}]}}}